/* Linker version script for WLX (Lister) plugin modules.
 *
 * A .wlx is a Total Commander / Double Commander Lister plugin: its host
 * only ever dlsym()s the "List*" C ABI (ListLoad, ListLoadNext,
 * ListCloseWindow, ListGetDetectString, ListSendCommand, ListSearchText,
 * ListSearchDialog, ListPrint, ListNotificationReceived, ListSetDefaultParams,
 * ListGetPreviewBitmap, and their *W Unicode variants). Everything else --
 * any vendored parser/renderer, the C++ runtime, statically linked helper
 * libraries -- is an internal implementation detail and must NOT be exported.
 *
 * Why this is load-bearing, not hygiene: Double Commander is itself a Qt (or
 * GTK) process, so its global dynamic-symbol scope already contains a full
 * toolkit stack and whatever libraries that stack links. If a plugin exports
 * an internal symbol that collides with one already in that scope, ELF symbol
 * interposition can bind the plugin's own intra-module call to the host's
 * copy instead of its own.
 *
 * This is not theoretical. markdownview vendors its own md4c (a build whose
 * md4c-html companion understands wikilinks) and links it statically, but
 * libQt6Gui.so.6 itself dynamically links the system libmd4c.so.0 (Qt uses
 * md4c for Markdown). With md_parse/md_html exported at default visibility,
 * the plugin's own md_html -> md_parse call became preemptible and bound to
 * the SYSTEM md4c, whose MD_SPAN enum / detail-struct ABI differs from the
 * vendored md4c-html the callbacks were compiled against -- dispatching an
 * ordinary link span into render_open_wikilink_span() with a null detail
 * pointer and segfaulting (confirmed live via gdb). Keeping every non-ABI
 * symbol local makes each intra-module reference bind to the plugin's own
 * copy, immune to interposition.
 */
{
  global:
    List*;
    ExtensionInitialize;
    ExtensionFinalize;
  local:
    *;
};
